      question: "What is Node.js? How does it differ from traditional web servers?",
      answer: "Node.js is a JavaScript runtime built on Chrome's V8 engine. It's event-driven, non-blocking, and single-threaded. Key differences: uses asynchronous I/O, handles concurrent requests with single thread using event loop, no waiting for I/O operations, better for I/O-heavy apps.",
      example: "const http = require('http');\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  // Simulate async database query\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    res.end('Hello World');\n  }, 10);\n});\nserver.listen(3000);\nconsole.log('Server running on port 3000');",
